/*
 * vendor-url-fetch — the one input shape this suite never had: a vendor
 * .deb that is DOWNLOADED.
 *
 * Protects: the design (a URL is a first-class build input) and
 * contract-brief.md rule 2 (two builds of one request are byte-identical)
 * for the code path that only a real download reaches.
 *
 * Why it exists. Every other vendor input in this suite is staged as a local
 * file path, so core/fetch's HTTP path had no covering row at all. Measured
 * on the 2026-09-07 matrix run, before this fixture: of the 35 evidence.json
 * files the run produced, ZERO contained a progress event. Three commits
 * landed against that path with nothing in the suite able to see any of
 * them:
 *
 *   - the progress-event determinism fix (core/engine's liveOnlyTypes),
 *   - vendor-URL redaction on the way into the signed bundle, and
 *   - the fetch-failure reason classification.
 *
 * The first of those had made EVERY build with a vendor URL unreproducible —
 * two bundle ids for byte-identical output — and it had to be found by
 * reading core/fetch, because the one fixture carrying determinism:true
 * resolves apt:jq, which apt downloads in its own private root, so
 * core/fetch never runs.
 *
 * What it asserts, and why each half is needed.
 *
 * 1. determinism:true, against a server that serves the SAME BYTES at two
 *    different speeds. checkDeterminism runs this request twice; the
 *    harness's RepoServer sends the first vendor response at full speed and
 *    paces every later one (httprepo.go's pacedFileHandler). core/fetch's
 *    progressWriter emits on a 250ms wall clock, so build one produces about
 *    two progress events and build two about ten. If those events reach the
 *    collector that becomes evidence.json — which the manifest hashes and
 *    the signature covers — the two builds differ by eight events and this
 *    row is RED. Serving both at the same speed would make the row green
 *    whether or not the drop existed, which is a green row proving nothing.
 *
 * 2. A credential-shaped query string on the URL, and BOTH directions
 *    asserted about it. evidence.json must contain "?REDACTED" (proving the
 *    redaction ran at all) and must NOT contain the token (proving it ran
 *    correctly). Either half alone is vacuous: the absence of a string is
 *    the normal state of almost every string, and a build that never fetched
 *    anything would satisfy it. lock.json carries the same pair because
 *    redaction there is a separate call site (redactOrigin) that a change to
 *    core/fetch alone would not fix.
 *
 * 3. The package actually installs and runs on the offline target, so the
 *    downloaded bytes are the ones that crossed the air gap rather than
 *    merely the ones that were hashed.
 *
 * KNOWN RED, and deliberately so: the lock.json half of assertion 2 fails
 * today. On its first run this fixture found that a vendor URL's query
 * credential is written VERBATIM into lock.json's packages[].origin.uri —
 * inside the bundle, hashed by the manifest, covered by the signature, and
 * carried across the air gap. evidence.json is clean, so this is the second
 * half of the leak 034f709 closed, in a place that commit did not know
 * about.
 *
 * The cause is two writers of lock.Package and only one of them redacting:
 * core/engine/lockbuild.go writes Origin: redactOrigin(sel.Origin), and
 * core/bundle/assemble.go's own buildLock then rebuilds l.Packages from the
 * same selections with a bare Origin: sel.Origin. The assembler's copy is
 * the one that reaches disk. redactOrigin's doc comment still says "No
 * backend populates Origin.URI with a live vendor URL as of this writing",
 * which stopped being true when attributeExternalOrigins landed.
 *
 * Reproduced outside this harness, in one container with the real binary and
 * no fixture involved: build with one http:// vendor URL carrying
 * ?token=..., then grep the bundle. lock.json leaks it; evidence.json,
 * debark.manifest.json and repo/Packages do not.
 *
 * The assertion stays. A row that is red because the product leaks a
 * credential into a signed artefact is this suite working, and weakening it
 * to recover a green tick is the exact trade this project has written down
 * that it does not make.
 *
 * The URL is http:// rather than https:// because the server is this run's
 * own loopback RepoServer and there is no certificate to trust. debark
 * honours http:// with an explicit warning (core/fetch's scheme switch),
 * which is the documented behaviour and is itself worth having a row over.
 *
 * Not matrixed across releases: the claim is about core/fetch and
 * core/engine, which do not vary by target release, and a second release
 * would buy a duplicate at the cost of another download. The release grid is
 * basic-install-matrix's job.
 */
{
  "schema_version": "debark.e2e.fixture/v1",
  "name": "vendor-url-fetch",
  "protects": "URL inputs; contract-brief.md rule 2 determinism over a real download; vendor-URL redaction",
  "tags": ["edge", "determinism", "fetch"],
  "determinism": true,
  "target": {
    "distro": "debian",
    "version": "12",
    "arch": "amd64",
    "installed": {}
  },
  "request": {
    "sign": true,
    "vendor_urls": [
      { "name": "acme-url-tool", "version": "1.0", "depends": "jq", "bin": true, "pad_bytes": 4194304 }
    ],
    "vendor_url_query": "token=DFE2E-QUERY-TOKEN-MUST-NOT-SHIP"
  },
  "expect": {
    "build": { "exit_class": "success" },
    "packages_present": ["acme-url-tool", "jq"],
    "binaries": [
      { "path": "/usr/bin/acme-url-tool", "exit_code": 0, "stdout_contains": "acme-url-tool 1.0" }
    ],
    "bundle_files": [
      {
        "path": "evidence.json",
        "contains": ["?REDACTED", "acme-url-tool"],
        "absent": ["DFE2E-QUERY-TOKEN-MUST-NOT-SHIP", "token="]
      },
      {
        "path": "lock.json",
        "contains": ["acme-url-tool"],
        "absent": ["DFE2E-QUERY-TOKEN-MUST-NOT-SHIP"]
      }
    ]
  }
}
